right invariants:** 2D RoPE when `S == H`, bottom-right commit equivalence, FSQ bijection/STE, delta prefix masks. 19/19 passing is meaningful for correctness, though not for quality.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Blocking findings (contradicted by your own report)\n\n### C1 — Self-speculative decoding is 4–10× **slower** than AR, with acceptance 0.0\n`modal_verify_report.json` → `benchmarks`:\n\n| Preset | Self-spec | Exact AR | Ratio | Acceptance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nano B=1 | 5315.6 ms | 1272.5 ms | **0.239×** | **0.0** |\n| nano B=8 | 5351.5 ms | 1235.1 ms | **0.231×** | **0.0** |\n| mini B=1 | 14991.8 ms | 1393.9 ms | **0.093×** | **0.0** |\n\nThe spec sells this as \"**8×–10.7× fewer weight sweeps**\" and \"lossless greedy equivalence.\" The lossless part is true (`exact_greedy_match: true`), but the speed claim is false — and at acceptance 0 it's not even fewer sweeps: each iteration pays K draft sweeps + 1 verify + 1 commit to emit **one** token (~10 sweeps/token vs 1 for AR).\n\n**Root cause is architectural, not a bug:** the draft is *concept codes → frozen Stage-1 decoder*, while the verifier is the *exact head*. These are different output distributions, so acceptance is near zero by construction. Self-speculation only pays when the draft is a cheaper approximation of the **same** distribution — e.g., an early-exit / fewer-loop pass of the exact head. **Either redesign the draft or delete the claim.**\n\n### C2 — PG-FIM's \"repair\" is a hardcoded Python template, and it fires 100% of the time\nFrom the ablation:\n\n- `repair_trigger_rate: 1.0` (every task)\n- `compile_rate: 0.0 → 1.0` **only** when repair is enabled\n- `sandbox_test_pass_rate: 0.0` everywhere\n- `mean_reward: 0.55` = `0.20 parse + 0.30 compile + 0.05 kw` (the `+0.50` test gate never fires)\n\n`_build_syntactic_keyword_repair` (`model.py:1443–1527`) synthesizes `_ok = 1  # kw` / `return a + b` from string templates and picks whichever parses. So the accepted output is **not** the model's parallel concept infill — it's a deterministic template. The concept path is a draft that is always discarded. This means the PG-FIM quality numbers measure the template, not the architecture.\n\n### C3 — Stage 3 GRPO learns nothing\n`final_mean_reward: 0.0`, `parse_rate: 0.0`, `compile_rate: 0.0`, `kw_faithful_rate: 0.0`, `avg_mean_reward: 0.0028`. With 4 generations and near-identical (zero) rewards, the GRPO advantage is ~0 → no useful gradient. `policy_param_delta_l2: 1.5` shows the optimizer moved, but toward noise. The pipeline is numerically stable (`all_logprobs_finite: true`) but **unvalidated as a learning method**. The reward is gated so hard (`test ⊂ compile ⊂ parse`) that a weak policy scores 0 at every gate.\n\n### C4 — Grammar masking guarantees *syntax*, not *semantics*\n`agent_verification` is damning in a subtle way:\n\n```json\n\"prompt\": \"Use calculator to compute 17 * 6 + 9.\",\n\"tool_call\": {\"name\": \"calculator\", \"arguments\": {\"expression\": \"15\"}}\n```\n\nSchema-valid, semantically wrong. The `JSONSchemaConstrainedMasker` enforces `{\"name\": <registered>, \"arguments\": <schema>}` but cannot make the *values* correct — and it removes the model's ability to abstain. \"100% schema-valid JSON\" is true but weak; the spec presents it as solving \"exact fidelity.\" Also note the mode switch is `force_tool:calculator` — a **router**, not a learned decision.\n\n### C5 — No baseline, no held-out quality metric\nThere is no comparison against a same-parameter AR transformer on validation loss/perplexity, no quality eval, no acceptance/quality tradeoff. Training is **1.4M / 1.5M tokens** — enough to prove the pipeline runs, not to conclude the architecture is better. The head-to-head table compares *paper specs of vanilla NCM*, not measured CNCM quality. **This is the single biggest scientific gap:** the thesis \"faster at comparable quality\" is unmeasured.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Correctness & implementation issues\n\n| # | Issue | Detail | Severity |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | **Stochastic self-spec proposal is invalid** | `_draft_concept_from_kv` samples FSQ digits with temperature but takes `dec_logits.argmax` for tokens (`model.py:967`). Acceptance then uses `q = softmax(draft_logits/T)` (`model.py:1355–1366`) — but the proposal is a point mass, not `q`. Exact only for `T=0`. | Medium |\n| M2 | **\"~100% grid utilization\" is a metric artifact** | `active_code_ratio = n_unique / codebook_size`; level 0 caps at `1/4096`, level 7 at `64/4096`. The average can't approach 100%. Reported 0.114 is batch-size-dependent. Use the existing `batch_utilization` (`n_unique / min(numel, codebook_size)`) and report per-level. | Medium |\n| M3 | **Head size / MAC claims overstated** | Measured: concept head = **71,072** params (nano), not \"~0.16M\"; flat-vs-factorized MAC ratio = **23.3×** (nano), **17.7×** (mini), not \">40×\". The 0.16M/40× figures only hold for `tiny_test`. | Low–Med |\n| M4 | **Early exit doesn't compose with batching** | Gate uses `mean_conf.min()` over batch (`model.py:958`). Report confirms: mini B=8 early-exit `exit_level: 8`, speedup 6.68× vs 6.60× — **no benefit**. One uncertain sequence blocks the whole batch. | Medium |\n| M5 | **Recursive looping saves weights only** | KV cache and attention FLOPs scale with *effective* layers (21/24), not unique (7/12) — your own 10.5 MB KV figure is 21-layer. The win is weight RAM/bandwidth, not KV or latency; 21 tied layers ≠ 21 independent layers in capacity. | Medium |\n| M6 | **Concept FLOPs go *up*, only weight reads go down** | `Σℓ_k = 147` target positions vs 64 AR positions → ~2.3× more position-layer FLOPs. The 8× win is weight sweeps. Correctly scoped to bandwidth-bound batch-1 decode in §5, but then used as a general speedup. B=8 shows 6.4× vs 6.8× — it already shrinks. | Low–Med |\n| M7 | **INT4 is slower in practice** | Report: int4 variant **492.6 ms** vs FP32 **200.0 ms** (2.5× slower) — `Int4PackedLinear` dequantizes then matmuls in PyTorch; 