re generations / a stronger base, or label Stage 3 \"experimental, no measurable learning at this scale.\"\n5. **Fix early-exit batching** (per-sequence exit / ragged levels) and **redefine the utilization metric** (`n_unique / min(numel, codebook)`).\n6. **Correct the doc numbers**: concept head 71K not 160K, 23× not 40×, \"5 innovations\" not 6, INT4 is memory-only, self-spec is slower.\n7. **Add a KV eviction/summarization policy** before claiming Android deployment.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Bottom line\n\nThe **core architectural bet is sound**: coarse-to-fine concept generation with zero-table FSQ, causal-prefix Block-AR, and a shared exact-AR escape hatch is a coherent, well-engineered design for bandwidth-bound edge decode. The **implementation quality is above average** — the mask discipline, dtype safety, and ONNX verification are real.\n\nBut the document reads like a *grant proposal*, not a *review-ready spec*: it claims acceleration for a feature that is 4–10× slower in your own measurements, attributes PG-FIM quality to the model when a template is doing the work, reports an unproven RL stage, and never compares against a baseline. **Fix the claims to match the evidence, run the missing baseline, and this becomes a strong paper.** Leave it as-is and a reviewer will find exactly the three contradictions above in the first ten minutes.\n\n```text\nResult: Reviewed CNCM architecture against its implementation and verification report; found strong core design but 3 headline claims contradicted by the repo's own evidence (self-spec slower, PG-FIM repair is a template, GRPO learns nothing), plus overstated metrics and a missing baseline.\nSkill used: none\nSkill created/updated: none\nFailure logged: no\n```"
