 --preset nano --data ... --max-hours 11 --resume auto --out /kaggle/working/ckpts`\n\n- Stops gracefully at `max_hours` (leaves 1h buffer), saves, prints `tokens_seen` and `val_loss`.\n- Sets `cudnn.benchmark=True`, FP16, one GPU.\n- Pre-tokenizes once to a `.pt`/memmap and reuses across sessions.\n- No internet during training.\n\n---\n\n## E. Expected numbers\n\n| Setup | Peak VRAM | Tokens/s (T4) | 300M tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `nano`, batch 24 (today) | 6.1 GB | 4–7k | ~17–21 h (1.5–2 sessions) |\n| `nano` + grad ckpt, batch 16 | ~3–4 GB | 3–5k | ~20–28 h (2 sessions) |\n| `micro`, batch 16 | <1 GB | 8–12k | ~8–10 h (1 session) |\n\nSo: **`nano` on T4 = 2 Kaggle sessions; `micro` = 1 session.** Both fit the 12h limit with resume.\n\n---\n\n## F. Order I'd implement\n\n1. **CheckpointManager + `--resume` + val loop** — mandatory for Kaggle, independent of architecture.\n2. **`GradScaler` + gradient checkpointing + 8-bit optimizer** — the 4 GB enablers.\n3. **int32 token stream + `micro` preset** — memory + a true low-end preset.\n4. **Trained BPE + `run_kaggle.py --max-hours`** — throughput and session safety.\n5. **Optional:** exact-loss scheduling, progressive levels, shared prefix pass.\n\n---\n\n## G. What not to do\n\n- **No DeepSpeed/FSDP** for a 14.5M model — overhead > benefit.\n- **Don't shrink `nano`** before the baseline says you must; 6.1 GB is fine on T4.\n- **Don't break the 19 tests** — checkpoint/resume, grad checkpointing, and `micro` should be additive flags, not rewrites.\n\n**Bottom line:** you can train `nano` on Kaggle today (it fits), but you can't do it *reliably* until resume + best-checkpointing exist. Add those first, then gradient checkpointing and a trained BPE; that's what turns \"it fits\" into \"it finishes in 12-hour chunks and keeps the best model.\""
