          "text": "## My preference, in one line\n\n**TinyStories for the quality run; a 40/30/20/10 mix (TinyStories / Wikipedia / permissive Python / tool-call JSON) for the capability run; a trained 4k BPE; 5% document-level holdout; pre-tokenized `uint16` memmap.**\n\nNot the current `make_synthetic_corpus` — that's a unit-test fixture generated from templates. Training and evaluating on it is circular, and a reviewer will spot it immediately.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Two runs, two purposes\n\nDo **not** try to prove quality and capability with one corpus. A 14.5M model trained on a 4-source mix will score worse on TinyStories than one trained only on TinyStories, and you'd be comparing it to published TinyStories baselines. Separate them:\n\n### Run A — \"quality\" (the paper-deciding run)\n\n| Source | Tokens | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `roneneldan/TinyStories` (train) | 285M | fluent prose at small scale |\n| held-out (document-level) | 15M | val loss / perplexity |\n| **Total** | **300M** | Chinchilla-optimal for 14.5M (≈20.7 tok/param) |\n\nWhy TinyStories: it was built specifically to show that 1–10M-parameter models can produce coherent English. It's the only corpus where a 14.5M model's output is *readable*, which is what makes the qualitative examples in the paper convincing. License: CDLA-Sharing-1.0 — fine for research with attribution.\n\nThis run gives you: held-out loss vs the same-param AR baseline, Stage-1 reconstruction quality, and generated samples a reviewer can actually judge.\n\n### Run B — \"capability\" (FIM, dual-mode, ablations)\n\n| Source | Share | Tokens | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| TinyStories | 40% | 120M | keeps prose from collapsing |\n| Wikipedia EN (`wikimedia/wikipedia`, 20231101.en) | 30% | 90M | entity/factual diversity, longer context |\n| Python, permissively licensed (`codeparrot/codeparrot-clean` — verify card) | 20% | 60M | PG-FIM, code mode, AST repair |\n| Tool-call / JSON (`Salesforce/xlam-function-calling-60k`, `glaiveai/glaive-function-calling-v2`) | 10% | 30M | dual-mode tool-call claim |\n\nDo **not** compare Run B's TinyStories loss to Run A or to published numbers. Run B exists to show the model isn't single-distribution and to support the FIM/tool-call sections.\n\n**If you can only afford one run: do Run A.** The quality-vs-baseline result is the one that decides whether the paper stands. FIM and tool-calls can be qualitative demos or cut.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tokenizer: train it first, or you waste 1.5× the compute\n\nThe current `CNCMTextTokenizer` is deterministic subwords + byte fallback — not trained on your corpus. On English it lands around ~2.5 chars/token; a trained 4k byte-level BPE gets ~3.5–4.0.\n\nThat's a **1.4–2× effective data multiplier for free**, and it changes the token budget:\n\n| Tokenizer | 300M tokens covers | Stage-1 recon |\n|---|---|---|\n| current deterministic | ~750 MB text | baseline |\n| trained BPE-4k | ~1.1 GB text | usually better |\n\nRules:\n- Train the BPE on a **100M-token sample of the union of both runs**, with byte fallback.\n- Keep `vocab_size=4096`. Going to 8192 adds ~1.5M tied embedding params (+10% of nano) for no meaningful gain.\n- **Both CNCM and the AR baseline must use the identical tokenizer and identical packed data.** This is non-negotiable for a fair comparison.\n- Decide now: changing the tokenizer invalidates Stage 1 and Stage 2 checkpoints.\n\n`CNCMTextTokenizer.from_hf_tokenizer()` already exists, so integration is a small adapter, not a rewrite.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Splits and contamination\n\n- **Document-level split**, not chunk-level. Chunk-level splitting leaks the same document into train and val and inflates your numbers.\n- **Dedupe**: exact hash + MinHash (threshold 0.8) on TinyStories — it was generated from templates and contains near-duplicates. Without this, a reviewer will call it memorization.\n- **Eval sets are eval-only**: TinyStories validation, HumanEval, MBPP. Check 13-gram overlap between your code tr