          "text": "## Short answer\n\nIt will **not** run on Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM, or anything llama.cpp-based — and that's expected, not a failure. CNCM is a custom architecture, not a Llama variant. It runs as a **small ONNX Runtime library** on desktop, browser, phone, and Raspberry Pi.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Ollama/llama.cpp can't load it\n\nGGUF requires the runtime to know the architecture. CNCM has four things llama.cpp has never seen:\n\n| CNCM component | Why GGUF can't express it |\n|---|---|\n| M-RFSQ concept tokenizer (FSQ + decoder) | not a transformer block; custom quantize/dequantize + decode graph |\n| Concept head (147 positions, per-level logits) | custom head with level-dependent shapes |\n| Per-level RoPE resets + loop adapters | non-standard attention indexing and weight sharing |\n| Two-pass decode loop (concept → chunk decode) | control flow outside a standard KV-cache decode |\n\nConverting would mean writing a C++ implementation of all four. That's 3–6 weeks and a maintenance burden, for a 14.5M model. Not worth it for a workshop paper.\n\nSame for vLLM/TGI: they support custom models via plugins, but the concept-mode loop isn't a standard `forward()` — you'd be writing a custom scheduler too.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What already exists in the repo\n\n`cncm/export/export_onnx.py` already emits three graphs:\n\n```\nstage1_decoder.onnx        # flat codes -> text logits\nstage2_exact_step.onnx     # input_ids + position_ids -> logits\nstage2_concept_step.onnx   # prefix + target codes -> digit logits\nexport_manifest.json\n```\n\nVerified against PyTorch at **5e-6** max diff in your report. That's your deployment foundation.\n\n**The gap:** `stage2_exact_step.onnx` calls `forward_exact(..., use_cache=False)` (line 123–127). So it recomputes the whole prefix for every token — O(n²) and unusable for real generation. There's also no runtime module that drives the concept loop or the tokenizer.\n\nSo you need one more piece: a thin runtime layer.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Where it actually runs\n\n| Target | Runtime | Artifact | Effort | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Windows/Mac/Linux** | ONNX Runtime (Python/C++) | 3 `.onnx` + `cncm_runtime` | 1–2 days | primary target; measured latency here |\n| **Browser** | `onnxruntime-web` (WASM, WebGPU EP) | 12 MB INT4 download | 2–3 days | good demo for the paper |\n| **Android** | ONNX Runtime Mobile (AAR) | same graphs, NNAPI/XNNPACK EP | 3–5 days | pin batch=1, bucket prefix lengths |\n| **iOS** | ORT Mobile / Core ML | same graphs | 3–5 days | Core ML conversion is the painful part |\n| **Raspberry Pi 4/5** | ORT ARM64 | same graphs | 1 day | best \"edge\" measurement for the paper |\n| **Server** | FastAPI + ORT | Docker image | 1 day | OpenAI-compatible endpoint if you want |\n| **ExecuTorch** | PyTorch Edge | `.pte` + custom ops | 1–2 weeks | custom ops for FSQ/concept head |\n| **MCU (ESP32 etc.)** | — | — | impossible | needs 22–30 MB RAM; ESP32 has 512 KB |\n| **Ollama / llama.cpp / vLLM / MLX** | — | — | 3–6 weeks | don't |\n\n---\n\n## 4. What to ship\n\nFour artifacts, in order of value:\n\n1. **`cncm/runtime/onnx_engine.py`** — loads the 3 graphs, owns the KV cache, implements `generate_concept()` and `generate_exact()`, handles sampling. This is the missing piece.\n2. **`demo/app.py`** — Gradio UI with a mode toggle (concept / exact) and a latency readout. This is what goes in the paper's demo figure.\n3. **`demo/web/`** — `onnxruntime-web` page. Strong \"runs in a browser, 12 MB\" claim.\n4. **`bench_edge.py`** — tokens/s, peak RSS, model size on laptop CPU + Pi/phone.\n\nFor the KV cache: add `past_key_values` as graph inputs/outputs to `stage2_exact_step.onnx` (the PyTorch path already supports `use_cache=True`, so it's an export change, not a model change). Bucket prefix lengths to `[64, 128, 256, 512]` so mobile doesn't recompile dynamic shapes.\n\n**Quantization:** do not ship the PyTorch `Int4PackedLinear` path — it's 2.5× *slower* (measured). Use ONNX QDQ / `MatMulNBits` with ORT's INT4 kernels, or ship FP16/INT8 if INT4 latency doesn't beat it on your device. Measure both.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Expected performance (projections, label them as such)\n\n| Device | Concept mode | Exact mode | RAM |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Desktop CPU (ORT, INT8) | ~50–150 tok/s | ~20–60 tok/s | ~40 MB |\n| Laptop browser (WASM) | ~5–20 tok/s | ~3–10 tok/s | ~60 MB |\n| Mid-range Android (ORT Mobile) | ~15–40 tok/s | ~8–20 tok/s | ~50 MB |\n| Raspberry Pi 4 | ~8–20 tok/s | ~5–12 tok/s | ~45 MB |\n\nThese are estimates from 14.5M params × 211-token concept pass; measure before putting them in the paper. The one number you already have is real: **6.4–8.85× faster than AR at batch 1 on an A10**.\n\n---\n\n## 6. What the paper actually needs\n\nOne measured device is enough. I'd do:\n\n- **Laptop CPU** (ORT, FP32 and INT8) — reproducible by any reviewer\n- **One phone or Pi** — proves the edge claim isn't a projection\n\nThen the deployment section reads: *\"12.3 MB INT4, 22–30 MB runtime RAM, X tok/s on [device], ONNX graphs verified to 5e-6 against PyTorch.\"* That's a complete artifact. No reviewer will ask why it isn't on Ollama.\n\n---\n\n## 7. If you want a chat-like UX\n\nWrap the runtime in Gradio or a FastAPI server with `/v1/chat/completions`. Any client can then talk to it — but be honest in the README: a 14.5M model is a demo, not a ChatGPT replacement. The value is the latency/memory profile, not the answer quality.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** Ollama is the wrong target for a custom architecture. Your deployment story is ONNX Runtime: three graphs you already export, plus a ~300-line runtime that owns the KV cache and the concept loop. Desktop + browser + Pi gets you a credible edge artifact; Android is a bonus if you have the time.\n\nWhen you switch out of Plan mode I can add the KV-cache export variant and `cncm/runtime/onnx_engine.py` first — that's what turns the existing graphs into something that actually generates.\n\n```text\n